  "query_preview": "/Tuesday through Saturday/...\nIssued 207 PM MST SAT NOV 14 2026\nWeather in the long term is seasonally typical in terms of \ntemperature, but with continual moisture supporting rounds of \nprecipitation through next week. Tuesday, temperatures finally cool \nto near normal as a weakening longwave trough moves through the \nregion. Down from 5-10 degrees above normal the day before, cooler \nair settles in for the week and drops snow levels to around 4-5 kft \nMSL. These higher snow levels will support a couple inches of snow \nin mountains and mountain valleys. In lower elevations, up to 0.2 \ninches of rainfall are possible. Precipitation heads east and the \nseasonal temperatures and cooler air mass move in. There is a very \nlow chance of snow in lower elevations with this system, as snow \nlevels aren't projected to fall to valley floors until Wednesday \nmorning; well after precipitation has ended. Conditions briefly dry \nout Wednesday as a ridge warms up the cold morning temps back to \nnormal that afternoon.\n\nHowever, Wednesday evening, a closed Pacific low pressure system \nbrings a more prolonged precipitation event. Precipitation with this \nsystem is most likely Wednesday evening through Thursday morning on \nthe eastern side of the low. Guidance suggests the core of the low \nwill track south of the forecast area, meaning as the peak moisture \nleaves the area, lingering wraparound moisture will continue \nprecipitation through at least Friday morning with isolated coverage \nthrough Saturday morning. Again, snow levels around 4-5 kft MSL will \nsupport a couple inches of snow on smaller mountains and in mountain \nvalleys. But, on the highest ridges and summits 6 to 10 inches of \nsnow become possible as snow ratios rise in colder temperatures and \norographic lift increases precipitation efficiency. Again, up \nto 0.2 inches of rainfall are possible in lower elevations. The \ncolder air behind the closed low only drops temperatures by a \nfew degrees, but enough for snow levels to approach valley \nfloors by Saturday morning. With a 10% chance of precipitation \nin trailing showers there is again a low chance for valley snow.\nA ridge will begin to build between the exiting low and a \nstrengthening closed low off the Pacific NW coast through the \nrest of the weekend.",
